      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Extreme Phoenix summer heat significantly affects bitumen by accelerating aging processes like oxidation and thermal aging. Prolonged exposure to high temperatures can cause the binder to lose volatile components, leading to hardening within the mix, even while it's soft on the surface. This chemical degradation reduces the binder's viscoelastic properties, making it less able to resist fatigue and thermal stresses over time. Therefore, it is likely a combination of periods of softening followed by permanent hardening of the asphalt binder.",
      "source": "target",
