    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you also look into the subgrade compaction side of things? we're worried there might be some uneven settlement underneath the overlay that's triggering these cracks. specifically, how would poor compaction in the lower layers manifest in relation to those thermal stresses?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Poor subgrade compaction creates zones of low stiffness, meaning the base or subgrade cannot uniformly support the pavement load or resist moisture infiltration. When combined with thermal stresses, these soft spots experience differential strain; one side of the pavement moves or settles more than the other. This differential movement imposes high, localized shear and tensile strains on the overlying asphalt, which is then easily translated into premature longitudinal cracking even under moderate thermal stress.",
